spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/APU_Envelope.md:6:The NES [APU](APU.md) has an envelope generator that controls the volume in one of two ways: it can generate a decreasing saw envelope (like a decay phase of an [ADSR](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synthesizer#ADSR_envelope)) with optional looping, or it can generate a constant volume that a more sophisticated software envelope generator can manipulate. Volume values are practically linear (see: [APU Mixer](APU_Mixer.md)).
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/APU_Envelope.md:8:Each volume envelope unit contains the following: start flag, [divider](APU.md#Glossary), and decay level counter.
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/APU_Envelope.md:37:When clocked by the [frame counter](APU_Frame_Counter.md), one of two actions occurs: if the start flag is clear, the divider is clocked, otherwise the start flag is cleared, the decay level counter is loaded with 15, and the divider's period is immediately reloaded.
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/APU_Envelope.md:39:When the divider is clocked while at 0, it is loaded with V and clocks the decay level counter. Then one of two actions occurs: If the counter is non-zero, it is decremented, otherwise if the loop flag is set, the decay level counter is loaded with 15.
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/APU_Envelope.md:41:The envelope unit's volume output depends on the constant volume flag: if set, the envelope parameter directly sets the volume, otherwise the decay level is the current volume. The constant volume flag has no effect besides selecting the volume source; the decay level will still be updated when constant volume is selected.
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/Errata.md:46:<tr><td>Sprites</td><td>Design</td><td>OAM uses DRAM (dynamic RAM), which needs to be refreshed frequently to avoid 'decay', the corruption of data stored in OAM. Accessing a byte of OAM refreshes an entire 9-byte row (8 contiguous bytes of OAM1 and 1 byte of OAM2), and all of OAM is accessed as part of sprite rendering on each scanline. This does not occur when the PPU is not rendering, but vblank is short enough that OAM will not decay during it. (Because of this, on lag frames, it is best to avoid OAM DMA because OAM will maintain its state and the OAM buffer in CPU RAM may have incomplete data that could cause garbage sprites or sprite flicker.) However, OAM can decay when rendering is turned off via <a href="PPU_registers.md#PPUMASK">PPUMASK</a> ($2001) for too long. Only the row pointed to by <a href="PPU_registers.md#OAMADDR">OAMADDR</a> ($2003) will persist, because the PPU accesses that address every dot. PAL systems avoid OAM decay during their much longer vblank by forcefully refreshing OAM during scanlines 265-310.<sup id="cite_ref-3"><a href="#cite_note-3">[3]</a></sup></td><td>
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/PPU.md:7:It has its own address space, which typically contains 10 kilobytes of memory: 8 kilobytes of ROM or RAM on the Game Pak (possibly more with one of the common [mappers](Mapper.md)) to store the shapes of background and sprite tiles, plus 2 kilobytes of RAM in the console to store a map or two. Two separate, smaller address spaces hold a palette, which controls which colors are associated to various indices, and OAM (Object Attribute Memory), which stores the position, orientation, shape, and color of the sprites, or independent moving objects. These are internal to the PPU itself, and while the palette is made of static memory, OAM uses dynamic memory (which will slowly decay if the PPU is not rendering).
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/PPU_OAM.md:62:The three unimplemented bits of each sprite's byte 2 do not exist in the PPU and always read back as 0 on PPU revisions that allow reading PPU OAM through [OAMDATA](PPU_registers.md#OAMDATA) ($2004). This can be emulated by ANDing byte 2 with $E3 either when writing to or when reading from OAM. Bits that have decayed also read back as 0 through OAMDATA. These are side effects of the DRAM controller precharging an internal data bus with 0 to prevent writing high-impedance values to OAM DRAM cells.[[1]](#cite_note-1)
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/PPU_OAM.md:119:### Dynamic RAM decay
spec/docs/nesdev-wiki/PPU_OAM.md:121:Because OAM is implemented with dynamic RAM instead of static RAM, the data stored in OAM memory will quickly begin to decay into random bits if it is not being refreshed. The OAM memory is refreshed once per scanline while rendering is enabled (if either the sprite or background bit is enabled via the [register at $2001](PPU_registers.md#PPUMASK)), but on an NTSC PPU this refresh is prevented whenever rendering is disabled.
